# Financial Markets: Where Savings Meet Borrowers

> Money that sits idle earns nothing; money that moves builds
> factories, homes, and retirements. Financial markets are the
> plumbing that channels savings to their most productive uses —
> through stocks, bonds, mutual funds, and the regulators (RBI,
> SEBI) who keep the pipes honest.

## 1. The Purpose of Finance

[definition]
**Financial markets** transfer funds from **surplus units**
(savers — households) to **deficit units** (borrowers — firms,
government) — directly (shares, bonds) through **capital markets**
or indirectly through **intermediaries** (banks) — in exchange for
returns: interest, dividends, or capital gains.
[/definition]

| Market | Trades | Maturity | Example venue |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Money market | Short-term debt | < 1 year | Call money, T-bills, commercial paper |
| Capital market | Equity + long-term debt | > 1 year | NSE, BSE, corporate bond market |
| Forex market | Currencies | Spot/forward | Interbank |
| Derivatives market | Contracts on underlying | Varies | NSE derivatives segment |

## 2. Equities: Ownership Shares

A **share** is part-ownership: entitlement to dividends (from
profits), voting rights, and residual claims. Prices aggregate
crowd beliefs about future earnings, which is why news moves them
instantly.

- **Primary market** — IPOs and follow-ons: the company itself
  receives the money.
- **Secondary market** — exchanges (BSE — Asia's oldest, 1875; NSE
  — 1992, electronic from birth) where existing shares change
  hands; the company gets nothing, but liquidity is what makes
  people willing to buy in the first place.

**Indices** summarise the market: **SENSEX** (30 BSE blue chips)
and **NIFTY 50** (NSE). Both are free-float market-capitalisation
weighted — bigger companies move them more.

[tip]
Price–earnings ratio (P/E) = price per share ÷ earnings per share.
A P/E of 20 says buyers pay ₹20 for each ₹1 of annual profit —
willingness to pay for growth, or overvaluation, depending on
whose story you believe.
[/tip]

## 3. Debt: The Bond and Its Seesaw

A **bond** is a loan in tradeable form: coupons (interest) plus
principal at maturity.

@math label="eq:yield-price"
\text{Price} \;\uparrow \;\Longleftrightarrow\; \text{Yield} \;\downarrow
@/math

Buy a ₹1,000 bond paying ₹80; if market rates rise to 10%, new
buyers demand ₹100 — your older ₹80 bond must be cheap enough (₹800)
to match. **Bond prices and yields move opposite ways** — the
single most-asked exam fact in finance.

| Instrument | Issuer | Risk |
| --- | --- | --- |
| T-bills / G-secs | Government of India | Lowest (sovereign) |
| State development loans | State governments | Low |
| Corporate bonds / CPs | Companies | Credit-rated, higher |
| Municipal bonds | Urban local bodies | Varies |

## 4. Intermediaries and Institutions

- **Banks** (see the Banking & RBI lesson) — the indirect channel:
  deposits transformed into loans, with maturity transformation and
  diversification.
- **Mutual funds** pool small savings: **NAV** = (assets −
  liabilities) ÷ units; equity funds, debt funds, hybrid funds;
  Systematic Investment Plans (SIPs) ride **rupee-cost averaging**.
- **Insurance and pension funds** (LIC, EPFO, NPS) — long-horizon
  money that owns much of the market.
- **Brokers, depositaries (NSDL/CDSL — demat accounts)**, and
  **market makers** keep the machinery running.

