## 5. The Regulators

| Regulator | Domain | Key statute |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **RBI** (1935) | Banks, money market, G-secs, payments, forex | RBI Act 1934, Banking Regulation Act 1949 |
| **SEBI** (1988 stat.; 1992 statutory) | Securities market: exchanges, brokers, IPOs, mutual funds, FPIs, insider trading | SEBI Act 1992 |
| **IRDAI** (1999) | Insurance | IRDA Act 1999 |
| **PFRDA** (2003/14) | Pensions (NPS) | PFRDA Act 2013 |

[important]
**SEBI's mandate:** protect investors, develop the market, and
regulate it — through disclosure-driven IPO norms (DRHP → SEBI
observations → listing), **Prohibition of Insider Trading
regulations**, **FPI registration**, takeover codes, and
surveillance (price bands, circuit breakers, ASM lists). The
**grievance redressal** SCORES system takes investor complaints.
[/important]

## 6. Instruments Beyond Stocks and Bonds

- **Derivatives** — futures (obligation) and options (right, not
  obligation): hedging for farmers, exporters, and fund managers;
  zero-sum speculation on the other side. Basis = spot − future.
- **InvITs/REITs** — fractional ownership of infrastructure and
  real estate income streams.
- **AIFs, PMS** — the sophisticated-money lane.
- **Gold (Sovereign Gold Bonds) and crypto** — alternative assets
  outside SEBI's equity world (crypto regulated as VDA under PMLA,
  30% tax).

## 7. Behaviour, Bubbles, and the Long Run

- **Compounding:** ₹10,000/year for 30 years at 12% ≈ ₹144 lakh —
  time in the market beats timing the market.
- **Bubbles:** South Sea (1720), dot-com (2000), and every
  "this time is different" — prices detach from earnings; SEBI's
  surveillance and margin systems try to soften, not abolish, the
  cycle.
- **Systemic risk:** one default cascading (IL&FS 2018, Yes Bank
  2020 rescues) — the reason RBI stress-tests and deposit insurance
  (DICGC, ₹5 lakh) exist.
- **Financial inclusion** completes the design: UPI made payments
  universal; the next frontier is bringing the last 200 million
  savers into formal markets via SIPs and NPS.

[summary]
Financial markets move savings to their highest-valued use: equity
markets (NSE/BSE) sell ownership, debt markets trade bonds whose
prices move inversely to yields, and intermediaries — banks, mutual
funds, insurers, pension funds — do the pooling. RBI governs money
and banks; SEBI polices securities with a
protect-develop-regulate charter; IRDAI and PFRDA complete the
oversight. Derivatives manage risk, indices summarise sentiment,
compounding rewards patience, and regulators stand between
exuberance and systemic collapse.
[/summary]
